# git server setup — real-world examples

## Set up your own bare git server over SSH

```sh
# On the git server (Debian/Ubuntu: apt-get; OpenSUSE: sudo zypper install git-core)
sudo apt-get install git-core
sudo useradd git
sudo passwd git
```

```sh
# On the local system: create a key and install it on the server
ssh-keygen -t rsa
cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub | ssh git@remote-server "mkdir -p ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys"
# (ssh-copy-id git@remote-server does the same in one step)
```

```sh
# Back on the git server: create the bare repository
mkdir -p /home/git/project-1.git
cd /home/git/project-1.git
git init --bare
# Initialized empty Git repository in /home/git/project-1.git/
```

```sh
# On the local system: start a repo and point it at the server
mkdir -p ~/git/project
cd ~/git/project
git init
git add .
git commit -m "message" -a
git remote add origin git@remote-server:/home/git/project-1.git
git push -u origin master
```

## Git global setup (once per machine)

```sh
git config --global user.name "John Doe"
git config --global user.email "johndoe@email.com"
```

## Create a new repository

```sh
cd Projects
touch README.md
git add README.md
git commit -m "add README"
git push -u origin master
```

## Push an existing folder

```sh
cd existing_folder
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git push -u origin master
```

## Push an existing git repository to a new remote

```sh
cd existing_repo
git push -u origin --all
git push -u origin --tags
```

## Homelab variant: bare repo on the homelab box, cloned anywhere

```sh
ssh david@homelab "git init --bare ~/repos/dotfiles.git"
git clone david@homelab:repos/dotfiles.git
```
